From 75f6f3dd7b2d196818278d22ec6dab048c3f865b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djrobson5 <256682087+djrobson5@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:14:36 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 18/19] Metal: command buffer GPU intervals

The queue's completed handler (trackInFlight) can record the GPUStartTime and
GPUEndTime of each command list it commits, under the list's label, into a
bounded ring that a caller drains (setGpuIntervalCapture, drainGpuIntervals),
off by default. It reports how long the GPU was busy without a timestamp: on
Apple GPUs a timestamp query ends the encoder and waits for all earlier work.

The label is the list's debugLabel (a string literal, default unchanged),
passed to trackInFlight when the buffer is committed, so the handler does no
Foundation work; the wait, acquire and present buffers pass none and are not
recorded. A list that only carries a fence can be given its own label, so the
caller can leave it out. The handler runs before endInFlight, so waitIdle still
covers it, and it is registered before the signal-fence handler of the last
list, so a waiter that sees the fence finds the interval recorded. Buffers
that failed or have no times are skipped. The list's NSString label is made
once, not at every begin().
